<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pharnavaz I of Iberia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 300 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnavazid-dynasty">Pharnavazid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pharnavaz I was the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, or Kartli, in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, according to the medieval Georgian chronicles, which place him around 300 BCE. They credit him with uniting the country after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, organising it into provinces under eristavis and creating the Georgian script, a claim modern scholars reject. His historicity is uncertain, but he became a founding figure of Georgian kingship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pharnavaz-i-of-iberia.jpg" alt="Pharnavaz I of Iberia" width="320" height="694" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaqeli · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pharnavaz_I_relief_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Pharnavaz I was a king of Kartli, the ancient Georgian kingdom known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in classical antiquity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartlis-tskhovreba">The Georgian Chronicles</a> credit him with founding the kingship of Kartli and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnavazid-dynasty">Pharnavazid dynasty</a>, while other chronicles, such as The Conversion of Kartli, make him the second Georgian monarch. Most scholars, going by medieval evidence, place his rule in the 3rd century BCE. He is not directly attested in non-Georgian sources, and his story is full of imagery from folk tradition. According to the c. 800 chronicle The Life of Kings, he traced his descent from Kartlos, the mythical ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>. His name is based on the Persian word farnah, the divine radiance that ancient Iranians believed marked a legitimate ruler.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the royal annals Pharnavaz lost his family at the age of three, when Azon, installed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, usurped his inheritance. After a dream and the discovery of a hidden treasure in a cave while hunting, he raised an army with the help of Kuji of Colchis and defeated and killed Azon, becoming king at 27. He is said to have acknowledged the suzerainty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, and to have patterned his administration on the Persian kingdom. The annals credit him with dividing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> among regional governors called eristavis, under a commander-in-chief, the spaspet. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> gives the Iberian army as 70–80,000. Modern scholars treat the reform as most likely a back-projection of the medieval pattern onto the distant past.</p><p>He is also said to have raised the idol Armazi on a mountain ledge, built a fortress of the same name, fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>, and rebuilt the cities destroyed by Alexander. He allied with North Caucasian peoples, marrying a Durdzuk woman and marrying his sister to a Sarmatian chief. The annals say he created the Georgian script and made Georgian the official language. They give him a reign of 65 years and say that he was buried in front of Armazi and worshipped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His son Sauromaces I succeeded him. According to the annals, Pharnavaz's grave is undisclosed, and one of the last kings said to have visited it was Mirian III. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a> claimed descent from him, and Georgian kings presented themselves as heirs of the kingdom he founded. Some modern scholars have identified him with the Pharasmanes whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> describes visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, though others suggest the Greek copyists confused two names.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pharnavaz I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.84,44.72&amp;year=-300" aria-label="Mtskheta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Pharnavaz I of Iberia"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5927 3934h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5927 3934h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.84,44.72&amp;year=-300">What was here in 300 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
